course: biology
lesson: 152
title: Myth check: do goldfish have a three-second memory?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People joke that a goldfish swimming round its bowl sees the same castle every few seconds and thinks «oh, a castle!» each time, because it forgets everything after three seconds.
explain: It is a myth: experiments show goldfish remember things for weeks and even months, and they learn quickly when there is food to be had.
explain: Watch a pet goldfish when you walk up to the tank: it swims to the front, because it has learned that you mean dinner.
explain: Fish need memory just like other animals, to find food, avoid danger and know their way around, and a fish that forgot everything every three seconds would not survive in a pond for a week.
fact: The three-second memory is a myth; experiments show goldfish can remember things for weeks and even months.
fact: In one experiment goldfish learned to push a lever for food and remembered the time of day when it worked.
fact: Goldfish quickly learn that a person near the tank means food is coming, and they gather at the front.
fact: Fish can be trained to come to a sound, a colour or a light that comes before food, and some fish farms use a sound to call fish.
fact: Fish can learn simple mazes and remember the way, sometimes using landmarks like stones or plants.
fact: Small round bowls are bad for goldfish: their waste quickly makes the water dirty and short of oxygen, so they need a big tank with a filter; a fish that keeps gulping at the surface may be short of oxygen.
fact: Goldfish do not «grow to fit the bowl»; in a small bowl their growth is stunted and they get ill.
fact: In a good pond goldfish can grow to over 20 to 30 centimetres and live 10 to 20 years or more.
fact: Goldfish must never be let go in the wild, where they can grow huge and harm native fish.
fact: Goldfish come from a wild carp in China, bred for their colours over about a thousand years.
fact: Goldfish have four kinds of colour cells in their eyes, while we have three, and they can see some ultraviolet light.
fact: Most scientists now think fish feel pain and stress; they learn to avoid things that hurt them, so many countries have rules about caring for fish kindly.
fact: Fish breathe by drawing water in through the mouth and over the gills, so a goldfish opens and shuts its mouth all the time.
fact: Fish rest and sleep, but most have no eyelids, so their eyes stay open.
exercise: Is it true that a goldfish forgets everything after three seconds? | No, goldfish can remember things for weeks or months | Yes, scientists measured exactly three seconds | Yes, but only goldfish kept in small round bowls, not in big tanks | No, it forgets after one second
exercise: What did goldfish learn in the lever experiment? | To push a lever for food at the time of day it worked | To open the lid of the tank | To count to ten | To switch off the tank light
exercise: Why do goldfish swim to the front when someone comes near? | They have learned that a person often means food | They are scared and want to escape | They are trying to look at their own reflection in the glass of the tank | The light is always better at the front
exercise: A keeper rings a bell before every feed. What happens after a while? | The fish come when they hear the bell, even before the food | The fish swim away from the bell | Nothing, fish cannot hear at all | The fish forget the bell every three seconds, so they never learn what it means
exercise: Why are small round bowls bad for goldfish? | The water quickly gets dirty and short of oxygen | The round glass makes them dizzy | They are too cold for fish | Goldfish hate seeing their own curved reflection all around them all day
exercise: What happens to a goldfish kept in a tiny bowl? | Its growth is stunted and it gets ill | It stays small but perfectly healthy, which is why bowls are fine | It grows to fill the bowl exactly, then stops | It learns to breathe air like a land animal
exercise: How long can a goldfish live in a good pond? | 10 to 20 years or more | Only about three weeks or so | About one year at the most | About one hundred years
exercise: Why must you never let a goldfish go in a river or lake? | It can grow huge and harm native fish | It will turn grey and sad | It cannot swim in moving water | Ducks will become ill
exercise: Where do goldfish come from? | A wild carp in China, bred for colour over about a thousand years | Gold mines in South Africa | Coral reefs in the ocean | Pet shops invented them a few years ago by painting small grey fish gold
exercise: How many kinds of colour cells does a goldfish have in its eyes? | Four | Three | Two | One
exercise: Can a goldfish see colours we cannot see? | Yes, it can see some ultraviolet light | No, it sees only black and white and grey | No, it sees exactly the same colours that we see, and no others | Yes, but only the colour gold, like its own scales
exercise: Can fish learn a maze? | Yes, they can learn a simple maze and remember the way | No, fish cannot learn anything | Only if the maze is gold | Only sharks can
exercise: Why does a goldfish open and shut its mouth all the time? | It is pumping water over its gills to breathe | It is talking to the other fish in the tank with little bubbles | It is always hungry and chewing on bits of food | It is trying to sing a quiet song to its friends
exercise: How does a fish sleep? | It rests still with its eyes open, because most fish have no eyelids | It closes its eyelids and lies on the bottom of the tank, like we do in bed | It never sleeps at all | It floats upside down every night
exercise: What do most scientists now think about fish and pain? | They feel it and learn to avoid things that hurt them | They feel nothing at all, because their brains are tiny | Only big fish like sharks feel it, never small goldfish | Only fish taken out of the water can feel pain, never fish in it
test: «my goldfish forgets me every 3 seconds so it doesn't matter if i forget to feed it» What is right? | Wrong twice: it can remember for months, and it still needs regular food | Right, it won't notice | Right, goldfish only need to eat about once a month, so a week is nothing | Right, goldfish feed on the bowl
test: A cartoon goldfish sees the castle in its bowl and gasps «new castle!» every lap. What is true? | It is a joke; real goldfish remember their surroundings for weeks | That is exactly how goldfish think | Real goldfish cannot see castles | Real goldfish remember only gold things, so they would forget a grey castle
test: «three goldfish, no wait, four. how many eyes is that» | 8 | 6 | 12 | 4
test: Two goldfish and a cat are watching each other. How many tails altogether? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 6
test: What would happen if a fish really forgot everything every three seconds? | It could not learn where food or danger is and would soon die in the wild | It would live much longer | It would get cleverer | Nothing would change for it, because fish live on luck rather than memory
test: «they grow to fit the bowl, so a small bowl keeps 'em cute and small» What is right? | A small bowl stunts their growth and makes them ill; they need a big filtered tank | Yes, that is how it works | Yes, and it makes them live longer | No, they shrink back down if they are put in a big tank, so small bowls are best
test: «we're moving, just chuck the goldfish in the lake, it'll be free» What is the best answer? | Don't; it can grow huge and harm the lake's own fish, so give it to a new owner | Fine, goldfish belong in lakes | Fine, it will die within a day anyway, so it makes no difference where it goes | Fine, if you do it at night
test: A goldfish darts away when the net comes in. What does that show? | It senses the net and avoids something that could hurt or scare it | It wants to play chase | It forgot what a net is | It is trying to swim into the net because it wants to be taken out of the tank
test: A goldfish in a science video swims through a maze faster each day. What does that show? | It remembers the way from earlier tries | It is just lucky every day | The maze gets shorter each day | Fish swim faster when tired
test: «stupid fish's staring at nothing again» Why might the goldfish be looking at «nothing»? | It may be seeing ultraviolet light that we cannot see | It is asleep and dreaming | It has forgotten how to swim and is waiting until it remembers | Fish are blind
test: Why might a goldfish keep gulping at the surface? | The water may be short of oxygen | It is very happy and wants to play | It wants to jump out of the water and fly | It is trying to drink the air for fun
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the duration of memory in the goldfish. | Experimental evidence indicates that memories may be retained for weeks or months | Memory is retained for a period not exceeding three seconds | The goldfish possesses no capacity for memory whatsoever, owing to the small size of its brain | Memory is retained solely in respect of the colour gold
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a small spherical bowl is unsuitable for the keeping of goldfish. | Waste accumulates rapidly and the water becomes deficient in oxygen | The curvature of the glass induces confusion in the fish | The fish is compelled to grow to the exact dimensions of the bowl and then cease growing entirely | The bowl admits an excess of daylight
exam: It is given that a fish keeper sounds a bell on each occasion prior to feeding. Determine the behaviour subsequently exhibited by the fish. | The fish assemble upon hearing the bell, in anticipation of food | The fish disperse upon hearing the bell | The fish exhibit no alteration in behaviour | The fish cease to feed altogether
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the domestic goldfish? | It was bred from a wild carp in China over approximately one thousand years | It is a marine fish of tropical coral reefs | It was produced artificially by breeders in Europe within the present century | It descends from a species of shark
